|~ What is declared under `::map`: the refusal of its constructor, and
   the verbs of maps, each with the parts of its args and its answer.
   A map's elements are its values, and
   its keys are the shape that travels with them: a verb that keeps the
   kind keeps the keys of the entries it keeps. ~|

:map/construct
  |~~ Reads a map as the bare map, and builds one from the vector of its pairs `[key value]`. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/PayloadNotMapError
  |~~ `::map` reads a map as the map it is and a vector as its pairs;
      a payload of another kind is refused.
  ~~|

:map/PayloadNotMapError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number payload is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::map(1) !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/PayloadNotMapError ::number])

:map/ElementNotPairError
  |~~ Each element of the vector `::map` builds from is a pair `[key
      value]`; `:index` names the first that is not.
  ~~|

:map/ElementNotPairError/refusesAPairOfOne
  |~~ An element of one place is no pair, and is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~([[:a 1] [:b]] | tag ::map !| [type /index] | eq [::map/ElementNotPairError 1])

:map/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key of a pair is a keyword, or a string or a number naming the
      keyword of its text; `:index` names the pair whose key is another
      value.
  ~~|

:map/KeyNotKeywordError/refusesAVectorKey
  |~~ A vector key is refused at its pair. ~~|
  ::law~([[[1] 2]] | tag ::map !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::map/KeyNotKeywordError ::vec 0])

:map/count
  |~~ The number of entries. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/count/countsTheEntries
  |~~ Three entries count three. ~~|
  ::law~({:x 1 :y 2 :z 3} | count | eq 3)

:map/count/size
  |~~ How many entries the map holds. ~~|
  ::number

:map/empty
  |~~ Whether the map holds no entry. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/empty/holdsOfTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map is empty. ~~|
  ::law~({} | empty | eq true)

:map/empty/vacant
  |~~ Whether the map holds no entry. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/first
  |~~ The value of the first entry, or null for the empty map. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/first/answersTheFirstValue
  |~~ The value of the first entry answers. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | first | eq 1)

:map/last
  |~~ The value of the last entry, or null for the empty map. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/last/answersTheLastValue
  |~~ The value of the last entry answers. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | last | eq 2)

:map/sum
  |~~ The sum of the values, each a number. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/sum/addsTheValues
  |~~ The sum adds every value. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 10 :b 20} | sum | eq 30)

:map/sum/total
  |~~ The sum of the values. ~~|
  ::number

:map/sum/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `sum` adds the numbers the map holds; `:key` names the first entry
      whose value is none, and a field is read first, `* /amount | sum`.
  ~~|

:map/sum/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the values is refused under its key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b "two"} | sum !| [type /actualType /key] | eq [::map/sum/ElementNotNumberError ::string :b])

:map/sum/ElementNotNumberError/key
  |~~ The key of the first entry whose value is no number. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/avg
  |~~ The mean of the values, each a number, or null when there is none. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/avg/answersTheMean
  |~~ The mean of one and three is two. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 3} | avg | eq 2)

:map/avg/mean
  |~~ The mean of the values, null for an empty map. ~~|
  #[::number ::null]

:map/avg/ElementNotNumberError
  |~~ `avg` takes the mean of the numbers the map holds; `:key` names the
      first entry whose value is none.
  ~~|

:map/avg/ElementNotNumberError/refusesAString
  |~~ A string among the values is refused under its key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b "two"} | avg !| [type /actualType /key] | eq [::map/avg/ElementNotNumberError ::string :b])

:map/avg/ElementNotNumberError/key
  |~~ The key of the first entry whose value is no number. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/min
  |~~ The first value in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the first value; the key of the entry is read by `entries`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/min/answersTheLeastValue
  |~~ The least value answers. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1} | min | eq 1)

:map/min/findsTheKeyByEntries
  |~~ The key of the least value is read by `entries`. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1} | entries | min ~(/1) | /0 | eq :b)

:map/min/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:map/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:map/max
  |~~ The last value in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the last value; the key of the entry is read by `entries`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/max/answersTheGreatestValue
  |~~ The greatest value answers. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1} | max | eq 3)

:map/max/findsTheKeyByEntries
  |~~ The key of the greatest value is read by `entries`. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1} | entries | max ~(/1) | /0 | eq :a)

:map/max/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:map/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:map/filter
  |~~ The entries whose value the predicate answers true for, their keys
      kept; the joint test of a key with its value reads the keys, `m |
      keys | filter ~(…)`, and `at` the value of each.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/filter/keepsTheKeys
  |~~ The entries kept keep their keys. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | filter ~(gt 1) | eq {:b 2 :c 3})

:map/filter/keepsNothingOfTheEmptyMap
  |~~ The empty map filters to itself. ~~|
  ::law~({} | filter ~(gt 0) | eq {})

:map/filter/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/filter/kept
  |~~ The entries whose values the predicate accepted. ~~|
  ::map

:map/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `filter` answers a boolean for the value of each
      entry; `:key` names the entry whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:map/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | filter ~(1) !| [type /key /actualType] | eq [::map/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError :a ::number])

:map/filter/ConditionNotBooleanError/key
  |~~ The key of the entry whose predicate answered another kind. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/every
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for every value. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/every/holdsOfEveryValue
  |~~ Every value is positive. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)

:map/every/holdsOfBothValues
  |~~ Both values are positive. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)

:map/every/asksTheValues
  |~~ A map is asked of its values. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b -2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0) | eq false)

:map/every/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/every/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/every/all
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for every value. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/every/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `every` answers a boolean for the value of each
      entry; `:key` names the entry whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:map/every/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | every ~(1) !| [type /key /actualType] | eq [::map/every/ConditionNotBooleanError :a ::number])

:map/every/ConditionNotBooleanError/key
  |~~ The key of the entry whose predicate answered another kind. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/any
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for some value. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/any/holdsOfSomeValue
  |~~ One positive value is enough. ~~|
  ::law~({:a -1 :b 0 :c 2} | any ~(gt 0) | eq true)

:map/any/holdsOfOneOfTwo
  |~~ One of two values holds. ~~|
  ::law~({:a -1 :b 2} | any ~(gt 0) | eq true)

:map/any/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/any/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/any/some
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for some value. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/any/ConditionNotBooleanError
  |~~ The predicate of `any` answers a boolean for the value of each
      entry; `:key` names the entry whose predicate answered another kind.
  ~~|

:map/any/ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
  |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused under the key. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | any ~(1) !| [type /key /actualType] | eq [::map/any/ConditionNotBooleanError :a ::number])

:map/any/ConditionNotBooleanError/key
  |~~ The key of the entry whose predicate answered another kind. ~~|
  ::keyword

:map/sort
  |~~ The entries ordered by their values, or by the value the key
      answers for each, their keys kept.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/sort/ordersByValue
  |~~ The entries order by their values. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 3 :b 1 :c 2} | sort | vals | eq [1 2 3])

:map/sort/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:map/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:map/sort/sorted
  |~~ The entries in the order the key ranks their values. ~~|
  ::map

:map/take
  |~~ The first count entries. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/take/takesTheFirstEntries
  |~~ Two entries of a map are its first two. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2 | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/take/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:map/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:map/take/taken
  |~~ The first entries. ~~|
  ::map

:map/drop
  |~~ The entries after the first count, in the order of the map; a
      key leaves a map by `minus`, which takes the keys to leave out.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/drop/dropsTheFirstEntries
  |~~ Dropping two leaves the last. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2 | eq {:c 3})

:map/drop/leavesAKeyByMinus
  |~~ A key leaves by `minus`. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | minus #[:b] | eq {:a 1 :c 3})

:map/drop/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:map/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:map/drop/rest
  |~~ The entries after the ones dropped. ~~|
  ::map

:map/at
  |~~ The value at the key, a keyword or a string, both naming the key
      as the map stores it, or null for a key the map lacks: the soft
      counterpart of the strict `/key` projection, which refuses a
      miss with `::proj/KeyNotInMapError`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/at/readsAKeyword
  |~~ A keyword reads its value. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | at :a | eq 1)

:map/at/readsAString
  |~~ A string names the key as a keyword does. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | at "a" | eq 1)

:map/at/answersNullForAMissingKey
  |~~ A key the map lacks answers null. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at :z | eq null)

:map/at/answersTheDefault
  |~~ The default answers for a key the map lacks. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at :z 0 | eq 0)

:map/at/chainsThroughNull
  |~~ Soft reads chain through null. ~~|
  ::law~({:a null} | at :a | at :b | eq null)

:map/at/key
  |~~ The key the call gives.

      The key of `at` on a map is a keyword or a string, both naming the
      key as the map stores it: `at :email`. A modifier is computed
      against the subject before `at` runs, so `at /email` reads the
      value of the field first and hands that value to `at` as the key;
      `:actualValue` holds what it handed.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/at/key/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number key is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | at 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/at/key/NotKeywordOrStringError ::number])

:map/at/key/refusesAProjectionForAKey
  |~~ A projection is computed at the call, and its answer is no key. ~~|
  ::law~({:email {:home "a@b"}} | at /email !| /actualValue | eq {:home "a@b"})

:map/at/key/readsTheKeyword
  |~~ The keyword reads the field. ~~|
  ::law~({:email {:home "a@b"}} | at :email | eq {:home "a@b"})

:map/at/key/NotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ The key is a keyword or a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::string)

:map/at/default
  |~~ The value answered for a place the subject does not hold, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 1)

:map/reverse
  |~~ The entries in the reverse order, their keys kept. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/reverse/reversesTheEntries
  |~~ The entries come in reverse order. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | reverse | vals | eq [2 1])

:map/reverse/reversed
  |~~ The entries in reverse order. ~~|
  ::map

:map/reduce
  |~~ The seed folded over the values by the reducer. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/reduce/foldsTheValues
  |~~ The fold adds the values to the seed. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 3)

:map/reduce/reducer
  |~~ The code that folds an element into the value so far. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:map/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/reduce/seed
  |~~ The value the fold starts from. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError
  |~~ The quote of `reduce` names one verb, which the fold calls with the
      accumulator as its subject and each value as its first arg,
      `reduce 0 ~(add)`; a quote of several steps, a literal or a
      name of no verb is refused.
  ~~|

:map/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError/refusesAPipeline
  |~~ A reducer of two steps is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | reduce 0 ~(mul 2 | add) !| type | eq ::map/reduce/ReducerNotBinaryError)

:map/keys
  |~~ The set of the keys, keywords in the one order: sorted by name.

      `keys` answers the keys of a map; a vector of maps answers them
      element by element, `* keys`, and a vector's own places are its
      indices, `/0`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/keys/answersASetOfKeys
  |~~ The keys are a set. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | eq #[:age :name])

:map/keys/ordersByName
  |~~ The keys sort by name. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | first | eq :age)

:map/keys/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number has no keys. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | keys !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/keys/SubjectNotMapError ::number])

:map/keys/keySet
  |~~ The keys of the map. ~~|
  ::set

:map/vals
  |~~ The vector of the values, in the order of the entries.

      `vals` answers the values of a map; a vector of maps answers them
      element by element, `* vals`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/vals/keepsTheOrderOfTheEntries
  |~~ The values come in the order of the entries. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | vals | eq ["Alice" 30])

:map/vals/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector has no values by key. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | vals !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/vals/SubjectNotMapError ::vec])

:map/vals/values
  |~~ The values of the map, in order. ~~|
  ::vec

:map/entries
  |~~ The vector of the pairs `[key value]` of the map, in the order of
      the entries, each key a keyword: the map with its keys as data, for
      a step that reads a key beside its value, as `[type payload]` reads
      a tagged value. `tag ::map` builds the map back.

      `entries` answers the pairs of a map; a vector of maps answers
      them element by element, `* entries`.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/entries/pairsEachKeyWithItsValue
  |~~ Each entry is a pair of its key and its value. ~~|
  ::law~({:auth {:port 3000} :db {:port 5432}} | entries | first | eq [:auth {:port 3000}])

:map/entries/foldsTheKeyIntoTheValue
  |~~ The key joins its value as a field. ~~|
  ::law~({:auth {:port 3000}} | entries * (unionOf /1 {:name (/0 | keyword)}) | eq [{:port 3000 :name "auth"}])

:map/entries/spellsTheKeyWithTheValue
  |~~ The key and its value spell one string. ~~|
  ::law~({:axios "^1.6.0"} | entries * ([(/0 | keyword) "@" /1] | join "") | eq ["axios@^1.6.0"])

:map/entries/filtersByKeyAndBuildsBack
  |~~ Entries filtered by key build back into a map. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :tmp 2} | entries | filter ~(/0 | ne :tmp) | tag ::map | eq {:a 1})

:map/entries/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector has no entries. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | entries !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/entries/SubjectNotMapError ::vec])

:map/entries/pairs
  |~~ The pairs of key and value, in order. ~~|
  ::vec

:map/has
  |~~ Whether the map holds the key, a keyword or a string, both naming
      the key as the map stores it, so the `keys | first | has _` chain
      composes.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/has/findsAKeyword
  |~~ A key held is found. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice"} | has :name | eq true)

:map/has/findsAString
  |~~ A string names the key as a keyword does. ~~|
  ::law~({:name "Alice"} | has "name" | eq true)

:map/has/key
  |~~ The key the call gives.

      The key of `has` on a map is a keyword or a string, both naming
      the key as the map stores it.
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/has/key/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number key is refused. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | has 42 !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/has/key/NotKeywordOrStringError ::number])

:map/has/key/NotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ The key is a keyword or a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::string)

:map/has/held
  |~~ Whether the map holds the key. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/union
  |~~ The entries of both maps, the other map's value winning a key both
      hold.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/union/letsTheOtherWin
  |~~ The other map's value wins a key both hold. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | union {:b 3 :c 4} | eq {:a 1 :b 3 :c 4})

:map/union/mergesTwoMaps
  |~~ The entries of both are merged. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | union {:b 2} | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/union/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/union/with/NotMapError
  |~~ The with is a map. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::map)

:map/union/merged
  |~~ The entries of both maps, the second one's value winning a key both hold. ~~|
  ::map

:map/minus
  |~~ The entries whose keys the other value lacks: a map's keys, or the
      keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword or a string, as `at`
      reads a key.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/minus/leavesTheKeysOfASet
  |~~ The keys a set names leave. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/minus/leavesTheKeysOfAVector
  |~~ A string in a vector names a key to leave. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2} | minus ["a"] | eq {:b 2})

:map/minus/keys
  |~~ The keys the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/minus/keys/NotMapOrVecError
  |~~ The keys is a map or a vector. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::map ::vec)

:map/minus/remaining
  |~~ The entries whose keys the other value does not hold. ~~|
  ::map

:map/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `minus` takes out of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the other value that is neither.
  ~~|

:map/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | minus [:a 1] !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::map/minus/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::number 1])

:map/inter
  |~~ The entries whose keys the other value holds: a map's keys, or
      the keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword or a string.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/inter/keepsTheKeysOfASet
  |~~ The keys a set names stay. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter #[:a :b] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})

:map/inter/keepsTheKeysOfAVector
  |~~ Strings and keywords in a vector name the keys to keep. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter ["a" :c] | eq {:a 1 :c 3})

:map/inter/keys
  |~~ The keys the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/inter/keys/NotMapOrVecError
  |~~ The keys is a map or a vector. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::map ::vec)

:map/inter/common
  |~~ The entries whose keys the other value holds. ~~|
  ::map

:map/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `inter` keeps of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the other value that is neither.
  ~~|

:map/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError/refusesAVector
  |~~ A vector among the keys is refused at its index. ~~|
  ::law~({:a 1} | inter [[:a]] !| [type /actualType /index] | eq [::map/inter/KeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::vec 0])

:map/raise
  |~~ Puts the map on the fail track as an error, the act the `!{…}`
      literal writes with its bang, so a query fails where its own check
      fails. `|` and `*` step around the raised error into its trail, and
      `!|` opens it to the error itself.

      Its tag is the one its value shows, the outermost tag over the
      map, then the one the map carries, then a `:kind` that names a
      tag, then `::error`, so an opened error raised again is itself.

      `raise` puts a map on the fail track.
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/raise/raisesTheSubject
  |~~ `map | raise` raises the subject, a map the step builds among them. ~~|
  ::law~({:kind :oops :message "boom"} | raise !| /kind | eq :oops)

:map/raise/raisesAMapTheStepBuilds
  |~~ A map the step builds is raised as well. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | {:kind :oops :value /} | raise !| /value | eq 42)

:map/raise/raisesAnOpenedErrorAsItself
  |~~ An opened error raised again is itself. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| raise !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)

:map/raise/renamesByATag
  |~~ A step that lays a tag over an opened error renames it. ~~|
  ::law~("x" | add 1 !| tag ::Renamed | raise !| type | eq ::Renamed)

:map/raise/guardsAValue
  |~~ A guard raises an error of its own tag when a value fails its check. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | if (gt 100) ~(/) ~({:kind ::TooSmall :value /} | raise) !| [type /value] | eq [::TooSmall 42])

:map/raise/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no map to raise. ~~|
  ::law~(42 | raise !| [type /actualType] | eq [::map/raise/SubjectNotMapError ::number])
